A||||5||theogre||adVISORy. Fixing a limp visor.||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||03-22-2001||07:38 PM||||Do your visors refuse to stay where you put them?<P>loosen the screw in the visor itself and pull it off the hinge stem.<P>check the stem and see if it's loose in the cast elbow. (The part that is screwed to the roof.)<P>I went over 6 visors last week and 4 of the 6 stems were loose in the casting. If you hold the stem near the tip and give it a twist it usually tightens up. (Plier marks near the tip of the hinge won't cause any wear to the buching in the visor.) Try to work some locktite into it to keep it tight. <P>take the hinge off the roof and hold it stem up. Work the locktite down into the elbow by wiggling the stem. once the locktite is in give the stem a good twist to tighten it up. USe the permanent type locktite.<P>I don't know how long it will hold but it's better than having it flop all over..... Will at least buy you time to find another good hinge.<P>If the plastic bushing in the visor is too messed up to fix then try some heat shrink tubing that just fits on the hinge stem. cover the area between the elbow and the narrow part of the stem. shrink the tube only enough to take up any slack.<P>I have a new article in the works which will have upolstery patterns for the basic visor. If someone has a wrecked pocket visor they can sacrafice I'll make paterns for it too. (The only SE/GT pocket visors I have are good.)<P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000001||85Josh||03-22-2001||08:29 PM||||theogre, I just wanted to say that as a new Fiero owner (less than a month),I really appreciate all the work you do for us. THANKS!<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4<BR>CAVALRY SCOUT!(stuck in Field Artillery)||||reg||1
